Date: Sun, 12 Dec 1993 00:10:28 -0600
From: "Phil G. Fraering" <pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu>
Subject: SPACE: sci.space reorganization passed...

(Long live Dorot oops, Ray! The Wicked Line Eater is dead!)

I thought I'd put my intended comments up here. I would have liked to
have left them for review for a while before I posted them, but I
don't know when the new setup will be implemented and this post then
shuffled off to sci.space.policy.

(BTW: The "this group/groups" in the last paragraph is meant to
refer to sci.space and its new subgroups.)

Phil

Here it is!

Just felt like writing this, now that I see that the reorganization
passed. Now, officially, the technical side of Space Exploration is
separate from the political side.

No matter how much people like George Herbert try to pretend that
science and politics exist in isolation, the vast majority of the
human race behaves otherwise. I wish science didn't depend on
politics. I wish that this country hadn't set up its space exploration
program to be dependant on politics to the degree that it is.

It is still extremely dependent, despite my wishes. I firmly believe
that this is the major reason that we aren't sending people beyond low
earth orbit and that two of the last three major unmanned inter-
planetary probe efforts were failures. Individually we were all free
to blind ourselves to the situation. Now such blindness is
institutionalized in the usenet hierarchy, which will probably
encourage the further propagation of the idea that basic physics is
behind our lack of progress.

If such a separation as people are pretending is possible had occured,
DC-X would probably have finished its test flight program instead of
sitting in a hanger while ARPA refuses to release the fraction of the
money the United States Congress allocated them to study SSTO
concepts, of which DC-X is the only known operating hardware for
studying (the rest being the ever-present paper studies and CAD
designs and simulations which currently outmass the hardware for just
about every program we have).

No separation has happened. By pretending otherwise, the likelihood is
high that there will simply be another twenty-one years or more
(especially if the inertia remains) of humans not going beyond low
earth orbit and major probes becoming fundamentally broken before
they reach their destinations.

Please carbon-copy any replies and follow-ups to me via e-mail. I'm
not sure I can justify the waste of time this group/groups seems to be
starting to become.

Phil


