Date: Sun, 12 Dec 93 16:07:48 -0500
From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: The Importance of Reading

Ray writes:
>Jeff Fabijanic writes:
>> What's to say that emerging technology won't allow us to realize that
>> we've been taking a nation (a world!) filled with potential artists,
>> musicians, and craftsmen, and making their success dependent on their
>> mastery of an arbitrary, ancient method of data storage.
>
>   So they can become starving artists or musicians?

If an artist's hip-comp accepts all her receipts, balances her accounts,
and keeps her well-informed of her current and projected wealth, what
need has she to do it all manually?

If one can be in instant contact with almost anyone on the planet, or
leave a visual/vocal message, don't written letters become less indis-
pensable to communication? If one can use "virtual primers" to actually
teach or walk one through any task one needs to learn, why bother with
static, often ambiguous hard-copy manuals? Words on a page are just one
way to keep important data. True, it's often been the best way for a long
time now, but tech changes and that may change with it. How many folks
do you see using horses to get to work nowadays?

Ray continues:

>   Reading and writing are ancient, yes, but that doesn't make them bad.

I *never* said that. I simply said that I felt that those skills might
be in the process of becoming a lot less indispensable to the success of
an individual.

>I think that written language is the first and GREATEST invention of mankind.
>Without it, we will still be in the caves.

Sorry, but I have to respectfully disagree with this. I think spoken
language is our "first and greatest" (and the concept of "culture" that
that implies) followed by our tool-making followed by our ability to paint
and draw. Reading and writing (particularly abstract, phonetic writing)
are, in comparison, new-fangled specialties of the above abilities.

I should make it clear that I like to write and I *LOVE* to read. In a
typical week, I usually read at least 1-2 new technical manuals/textbooks,
a half-dozen novels, the whole daily Boston Globe, much of the NY Times,
the WSJ, other miscellaneous papers, and get in a fairly complete reading
of MacWeek, ADWeek, and 3-4 trade journals/magazines of particular interest
to my vocations and avocations. I also peruse many of the dozen or so
magazines that I and my housemates receive each week (which given our
various backgrounds, can make for some wonderful cross-pollination). And
I spend a couple hours a day reading and responding to email and UseNet
newsgroup posts. I have no intention of ever giving up my love affair with
the written word - I just don't think that this one skill will be an
overwhelming necessity to success for future humans.

(I also listen to Talk Of The Nation and several hours of local college
radio daily, CDs, tapes, a few regular TV shows a week, CNN, other cable
offerings, 3-4 movies a week, and 1-2 concerts/shows a month. And I spend
at least a total of an hour a day on the phone with friends and business
contacts, and at least that much time in person with friends/clients/etc.
This visual and aural info-gathering, far from making me less desirous of
the printed word, actually ends up inspiring a lot of new reading.)

Oh, and I learned to read on my own at age 3 (as did all my sibs, except
for Beth, now eight, who learned to recognize her name at 18 months and
was writing by age two). Unfortunately for those on the list that would
like to draw some cause-and-effect between a lack of early TV and the onset
of reading ability, I have always lived with television (I remember watching
the first episode of Sesame Street as a four-year-old). But my parents taught
me and my sibs to think, question and discover things on our own. Books, TV,
radio and the people and the world around us were all fair game.

Specialization is for insects.

 - jeff


